// DiagnosticDriverKinds.def — Driver / front-end command-line diagnostics.
// Included by DiagnosticMessages.def.
//
// These are the only diagnostics with nothing to point at: the driver or the
// front end (plang -pc1, reached directly or re-invoked as a subprocess by
// the driver) is reporting on the command line or on the toolchain, not on a
// place in a Pascal source file.  The driver's DiagnosticPrinter writes them
// under the program name in place of a file, line and column — "plang:
// error: no input files".  The front end's does not: like clang -cc1, it
// prints "error: ..." with no program-name prefix, the same as it already
// did for err_file_not_found — see the DriverDiagnostics tests that check
// the driver and the front end agree on that message except for the prefix.
//
// A missing input file is not here.  The scanner already catalogs that as
// err_file_not_found, with the wording the driver was using anyway, and one
// message for one condition is the point of a shared catalog.

DIAG(err_no_input_files, Error, "no input files")
// The front end's own complaint when given more than one bare filename.
// The driver never causes this: multi-file mode re-invokes the front end
// once per input, one bare filename apiece.
DIAG(err_multiple_input_files, Error, "only one input file is supported")
// A path that exists but names a directory, not a file -- passed where an
// input file is expected.  Without this check the front end went on to try
// reading it as source, which succeeds at open() but reads back empty,
// producing a cascade of unrelated "expected X, got end of file" parser
// errors instead of one diagnostic naming the real problem.  Issue #275 was
// this same gap in the front end's own entry point: issue #125/#134 added
// the check (and this diagnostic) only to the driver's, which a direct
// "plang -pc1 somedir/" never passes through.
DIAG(err_is_a_directory, Error, "is a directory, not a file: '%0'")
// -o names the same file as one of the inputs (issue #148).  Compared on
// resolved (canonical/absolute) paths, not the literal command-line
// spellings, so "plang foo.pas -o ./foo.pas" is caught too -- and checked
// before any compilation or writing starts, so the source is never
// truncated out from under itself.  Wording matches gcc/clang's
// "fatal error: input file ... is the same as output file".
DIAG(err_input_output_same, Error,
     "input file '%0' is the same as output file")
// The front end's own -o, or a dump mode's implicit output file, named a
// path that could not be opened for writing.
DIAG(err_cannot_open_output_file, Error, "cannot open output file '%0'")
DIAG(err_arg_requires_value, Error, "%0 requires an argument")
// -o given an explicitly empty value (issue #286), e.g. "plang -o "$X" foo.pas"
// where $X is unset -- a shell passes that through as a real, if empty,
// argument.  Opts.outputFile also uses "" as its own sentinel for "-o was
// not given at all" (see its doc comment in Driver.h), so without this check
// an empty -o was indistinguishable downstream from no -o, and silently fell
// back to the default output name.  Wording matches gcc's own refusal of the
// same command line.
DIAG(err_empty_output_filename, Error, "output filename may not be empty")
DIAG(err_unknown_dialect, Error,
     "unknown Pascal dialect '%0'; valid dialects: %1")
DIAG(err_dialect_not_implemented, Error,
     "dialect '%0' is not yet implemented; implemented dialects: %1")
// -ferror-limit=<N>: N was not all-digits (including empty, or a leading
// '-'), so there is no number to parse at all.
DIAG(err_ferror_limit_not_a_number, Error, "-ferror-limit= requires a number")
// -ferror-limit=<N>: N was all-digits but too big for ErrorLimit's unsigned
// range.  Caught with std::from_chars's own error code rather than
// std::stoul, which would throw std::out_of_range under this project's
// -fno-exceptions build.
DIAG(err_ferror_limit_too_large, Error,
     "-ferror-limit= value '%0' is too large")

// Toolchain: plang drives llc and a linker.  On ELF targets that is ld.lld,
// linking against the C runtime that the host GCC installation provides the
// startup files for; on macOS it is the system ld, linking against the SDK.
DIAG(err_no_gcc_installation, Error,
     "could not detect GCC installation; set PLANG_GCC_DIR to override")
DIAG(err_no_c_library_dir, Error,
     "could not find system C library directory; set PLANG_LIB_DIR to override")
DIAG(err_no_macos_sdk, Error,
     "could not find the macOS SDK; install the Xcode command line tools "
     "with 'xcode-select --install', or set SDKROOT to override")
DIAG(err_cannot_find_program, Error, "cannot find '%0': %1")
DIAG(err_program_exec_failed, Error, "failed to execute '%0': %1")
// A subprocess that died on a signal rather than exiting.  Without this the
// driver returned llvm's -2 as its own status -- exit 254 and not one word of
// output -- so a compiler crash looked like a compiler that had quietly
// decided not to.  A silent failure is the one outcome worse than a loud one.
DIAG(err_program_crashed, Error, "'%0' crashed: %1")
DIAG(err_cannot_create_temp_file, Error, "cannot create temporary file: %0")

// An argument beginning with '-' that no option in Options.def matches.  A
// warning rather than an error because it costs nothing to carry on without
// it, and because a build system passing a flag plang does not know is a
// common enough thing that failing on it would be unkind.  -Werror still
// makes it fatal, which is the point of it having a name.
// TRANSLATORS: keep the spelling "unrecognized".  The warning this belongs
// to is named after its enumerator, so it is turned off with
// -Wno-unrecognized-argument; a message spelling it the other way would
// send a reader to a flag that does not exist.  A command line is syntax
// and is not translated, even between two spellings of English.
DIAG(warn_unrecognized_argument, Warning, "unrecognized argument '%0'")
// -Wno-<name> or -W<name> where <name> answers to no cataloged warning.
// Cataloged as its own warning, like warn_unrecognized_argument above, so
// that -Werror still makes it fatal and -w or -Wno-unknown-warning-name
// still silences it -- issue #276: this used to be an unconditional
// std::cerr print in the front end that neither honoured.  Only the front
// end reports this: the driver parses the same -W options but leaves
// naming an unknown one to the front end, so it is not said twice (see
// Driver::configureDiagnostics).
DIAG(warn_unknown_warning_name, Warning, "unknown warning '%0'")
